Jacob decided to surprise his parents  that here here's a sophomore at Lewis &amp;  Clark University in Portland Mia  convinced his mom that he was going to  stay there for spring break but that  Friday he showed up at home to spend the  whole week with his family now it should  have been a great week restful relaxing  but Saturday night as he walked home  with some friends a man pulled up  demanded to know Jacobs gang affiliation  and shot him three times before he could  answer that was homicide number 12 last  year in San Francisco by year end there  have been 68 others there were 108 rapes  burglaries assaults thefts all in for  every 100,000 people in the city there  were more than 5,000 crimes and that was  right at the US average but there are  men and women working really hard on  this more than a million officers and  fifty billion dollars in operating  budgets and these are the numbers that  we hold our Police Department's  accountable to crime per capita is their  net income this is their main success  metric great right well actually there's  a really big problem with that see crime  is impacted by all sorts of things  outside of a departments control things  like population density education income  as a result our main way of measuring  the success of a police department isn't  actually very good at telling us how  they're doing as such our departments  are out there doing pretty dramatically  different things financially  strategically tactically with no real  way to say what's working and what is it  this time last year while it's still in  consulting either chance to partner with  Dallas Police Department to tackle this  problem and after six months of coming  through annual reports of doing a lot of  statistical analysis and interviewing  chiefs and officers across the nation  we're actually able to  to isolate some of the impact that  police department's do have on crime and  what we uncovered was a fascinating  story about incentives and impact at the  highest level a police department can  spend its resources in two very basic  ways you've got crime response now these  are things like your 911 response teams  and your entire investigations unit and  then you have crime prevention which is  things like your community engagement  and all your patrol officers now besides  crime per capita we actually hold our  department strictly accountable to a  couple other measures one of the main  ones is response times which is how long  it takes an officer to get to your door  after you call 911 what's really true  about that as a metric well it's  entirely crime response oriented and  sure enough we hold our departments  almost exclusively accountable to the  crime response side but what we  uncovered as we started to isolate that  impact was that it's actually a focus on  crime prevention that's really making a  difference and as our standards on these  metrics have gotten higher and higher  over time we've essentially created a  system where we're forcing our  departments to prioritize the side of  their business that doesn't contribute  to the bottom line now this tension is  very real on a day-to-day basis imagine  with me that you're a patrol officer  it's 11pm and you're on your beat in  your neighborhood you've been on  assignment here for over a year and  you've actually really gotten another  community that's actually why you're  here tonight when your contacts call  there's a little bit of unrest some  unresolved dispute and potentially a  high chance of violence now crime  prevention at it's very best you're out  there knocking on doors meeting with  community members keeping the peace and  then your radio goes off there was a  theft a couple blocks down it's nothing  serious maybe a couple iPods a PC but  someone just called it in because they  need a police report so they can file  for insurance now you don't want to go  but now that that call has come in  that response time clock is ticking and  you know it's going to show up in your  review and that's going to show up in  your sergeants and his lieutenants so  you go and when you get there you get  another call and then another one  officer I talked to describe this as  being a slave to the radio now who knows  what happened back in your neighborhood  that time maybe it was nothing or maybe  that was Jacob's neighborhood maybe it  was that night you wanted to be there  but unfortunately you had a job to do  this is the tension between crime  response and crime prevention and too  often it ends up being a tension between  making the grade and making an impact so  how do we fix it well let me tell you  about Chief Ed Flynn chief Flynn is one  of those grizzled old men of the law  he's all right angles whether it be its  personality or that freshly starched  uniform this is a guy who probably  belongs in Gotham City but in 2008 he  landed in milwaukee as a new chief of  police of one of the nation's most  dangerous cities now chief Flynn made it  clear on day one that prevention is his  big priority see the chief believes in  what he calls the 6010 rule and that's  at ten percent of criminals and  neighborhoods are responsible for sixty  percent of crime his message to his  officers was crystal clear no your ten  percent find the underlying issues  beneath these pockets of chron and solve  them rude amount and things changed in  his first year the number of traffic and  pedestrian stops in Milwaukee doubled  but during the same time period the  number of tickets written was actually  cut in half see officers were being  proactive with their community but they  weren't chasing tickets they were  building relationships this wasn't stop  and frisk this was stopped and asked how  are things going how are things in your  neighborhood how can I help especially  in those ten per cents  first even though there were so many  more interactions the number of citizen  complaints that year actually went down  then crime plummeted especially when you  control for some of those variables from  2007 to 2011 crime went down everywhere  in the US by about fifteen percent in  Milwaukee it was more than 30 that's  thirty percent less burglaries thirty  percent less theft thirty percent less  murder well what's crazy is if you go  back and you read the newspaper  headlines from this period you get a  completely different narrative response  times go up in Milwaukee and that's the  rub the metrics that we have in place  are at best incomplete but they're  probably just wrong sure chief Flynn can  plow through it by sheer force of  personality but what about the rest of  our departments we need change we need a  new paradigm for thinking about policing  for our city halls and our Chiefs that  means redesigning these metrics such  that they're aligned with the  department's mission metrics that  reinforce prioritizing the right things  but that's not all see in those cities  that I saw where they were really having  an impact on crime prevention was never  just a department activity it was a  partnership with the community with us  the fact is there's something that every  single one of us in this room can do as  well an engaged community that views the  police as a partner is the most powerful  tool in fighting crime so what I'm  asking is really pretty simple as  community members as community leaders  get to know your departments at home at  work at school learn what their  priorities are no your ten percents and  ultimately become a partner in  prevention so that you can help your  departments stop fighting crime and  start stopping it thank you  you
 

